{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["In bridge engineering, past survival often implicitly relies on inductive confidence in material consistency, whereas Popper explicitly denies this consistency for natural laws.","Chess heuristics often rely on statistical win-rates (inductive probability), whereas Popperian corroboration is strictly non-probabilistic.","The cost of error in physical testing is catastrophic failure, whereas in Popperian science the 'cost' is merely the loss of a hypothesis, which is viewed as a gain in knowledge."],"overturn_conditions":["If it can be shown that acting on a theory necessarily entails an expectation of its future success, then the methodological distinction collapses into practical induction.","If no coherent definition of 'severity' exists that does not implicitly rely on the uniformity of nature."],"shared_structure":["Selection of the option that has survived the most rigorous attempts at failure.","Decision based on maximizing information gain from potential future failure rather than predicting success.","Explicit rejection of the idea that past survival guarantees future performance."],"source_memory_refs":["bridge_stress_test_protocol","chess_opening_selection_heuristic"],"transfer_claims":["Just as an engineer tests the bridge design that has survived the most extreme simulations not because it is guaranteed to hold, but because its failure would reveal the most about structural limits, scientists prefer corroborated theories to maximize the severity of the next test.","The preference is a rule of procedure (how to play the game of science) rather than a statement of fact (the state of the board)."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms not as an inductive inference that the theory is more probable, but as a non-inductive, methodological rule to maximize the potential for falsification (learning), accepting the cost that we can never rationally justify the belief that the preferred theory will succeed in the future.","counterconditions":["If corroboration is interpreted as increasing the subjective probability of future success, the argument collapses into the induction Popper rejected.","If the scientist acts on the theory believing it is 'true' or 'likely true' rather than merely 'the best available target for criticism', the psychological state smuggles in induction.","If the concept of 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) is required to ground the preference, the argument fails because Popper's formal definition of verisimilitude was logically untenable (as shown by Tichý and Miller)."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Popper distinguishes between the logical status of a theory (which remains unproven and improbable) and the methodological decision of which theory to test next. Preferring the highly corroborated theory is a strategic choice because it has withstood severe tests, meaning it has high empirical content. By choosing it, we subject ourselves to the highest risk of refutation. If it fails, we learn more; if it survives, our knowledge grows. This avoids induction because we do not claim the past success predicts future success (probability); we only claim that testing the 'boldest' conjecture currently available is the most efficient engine for error elimination.","optional_refs":["8653a93a35d43191d6518c7629ca6b147480c93101961b1f816e7f6208016204","0e7c744798ee87399a050abd6cd2e04b6bc10eca0bf07854be5e563dd7989460"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85}],"simulation_proposals":[]}